.TH CTPOOL_FUTURE_FREE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_future_free \- release the caller's reference to a future
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_future_free(ctpool_future *f);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Drops one reference to the future. When the reference count reaches
zero, the library frees the future object with the standard free().
Three kinds of holder release a reference: the caller, the worker, and
every ctpool_future_get call that is still in progress. For a detached
future the producer holds one in place of the worker.

This never touches the void* result value of the task function, which
this module never allocates, copies or frees. See ctpool_future_get(3)
for who owns the result.

Call this exactly one time for each successful ctpool_submit_future,
ctpool_try_submit_future, ctpool_timed_submit_future or
ctpool_future_create_detached call, whether or not anything called
ctpool_future_get.

Calling it with no ctpool_future_get at all gives fire-and-forget
behavior: the task still runs, and the library frees the future by
itself when the worker finishes, discarding the result.

You can call this while other threads are inside ctpool_future_get on
the same future. Each of them holds a reference of its own, so the
library destroys the future only after the last of them leaves.

This call ends the caller's claim on f, in the same way as when a thread
gives up its last reference. The caller must not use f again after this
call, and must not hand f to a thread that is not yet inside
ctpool_future_get.

A call to ctpool_future_free with a NULL pointer does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
f   Future handle
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_submit_future (3),
